 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The server pushes each committed revision to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, giving fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around the socket approach: the change store keeps replayable revisions, delivery owns connection and session management plus reliable publication of committed revisions, and browsers reconnect and catch up after a dropped connection. The choice accepts owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior rather than paying a realtime vendor and coupling the board to it.
 
